## Explicit limits and proposed owning checks

The proposal narrows program-level coverage to explicit runtime
declarations and module execution, with one demonstrated unrelated body
excluded. Most class bodies remain protected as a unit. This is an honest
limit of the current ownership shape. It does not claim that class-specific
churn is solved. Further splitting requires a separately reviewed pure
extraction, rather than an invented private-method omission.

Imported implementation changes, mutable imported data, dynamic dispatch
inside imported code, injected ports, and superclass behavior are not proved
by these declaration seals. Their binding/source identities are protected,
and their current independent whole-file seals and runtime witnesses stay.
This is the same dependency-implementation limitation as a whole-file seal.
The explicit same-module sets above avoid an unreviewed attempt to infer
semantic dependencies from string matches or direct-this references.

After checker agreement, the bounded implementation is in
scripts/security-entry-points.mjs and scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs,
plus the normal PD-2 note/manifest. No production extraction is necessary
for this conservative version. All other seal records and route inventory
logic remain unchanged. Proposed meaningful checks are:

1. Existing entry-point inventory and controls pass unchanged.
2. A real protected router or gate/helper edit changes its named seal and
   fails the intended assertion, even with unrelated declarations unchanged.
3. A transitive same-module helper change, import-source/binding rewrite,
   or top-level initializer change fails the correct record.
4. Missing/duplicate selectors and let/var versus const changes fail closed.
5. A display-only entryLine body edit preserves the named gate seals and
   inventory. The whole-file baseline would detect that edit; the exemption
   must therefore be part of the checker's explicit agreement.
6. A class field/constructor/private helper change still changes its full
   class declaration seal. Both object review categories use the new record.
7. One ordinary gate at the final exact head and exact-head checker review.

These are proposed witnesses only. No check, control, test, gate, new
declaration seal, or runtime execution was performed for this proposal.
